-- Q1: medium / L3 — Forfeiture after Section 83(b) election
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Brightfield Corp grants Director of Sales Marco Diaz 5,000 shares of restricted stock on April 1, 2026, when FMV is $18 per share. Diaz files a timely Section 83(b) election and pays tax on $90,000 of ordinary income. He leaves the company before the shares vest and forfeits all 5,000 shares. What is the tax consequence of the forfeiture?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Diaz cannot recover the tax previously paid on the $90,000 of recognized income", "Diaz may deduct $90,000 as an ordinary loss in the year of forfeiture", "Diaz may file an amended return for the year of the election to recover the tax paid", "Diaz recognizes a $90,000 capital loss in the year of forfeiture"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 83(b). When a taxpayer makes a Section 83(b) election and subsequently forfeits the stock, the tax paid on the election is not recoverable. No deduction or loss is allowed for the amount previously included in income. This is the inherent risk of the 83(b) election.
Wrong (B): No ordinary loss deduction is permitted on forfeiture of property for which an 83(b) election was made.
Wrong (C): The IRS does not allow amended returns to reverse a valid 83(b) election upon forfeiture.
Wrong (D): Forfeiture of 83(b) elected stock does not generate a capital loss since no sale or exchange occurred.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L2 — SE tax base 92.35% factor
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Rosa Gutierrez, a self-employed consultant, has $120,000 of net self-employment earnings for 2026. What amount does she use as the base for computing self-employment tax?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$120,000", "$110,820", "$100,000", "$60,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 1402(a). The SE tax base is net SE earnings multiplied by 92.35% (100% minus the 7.65% employer-equivalent portion). $120,000 x 0.9235 = $110,820. This adjustment approximates the employer half of FICA that would be excluded from the wage base for employees.
Wrong (A): The full $120,000 is not used; the 92.35% adjustment must be applied first.
Wrong (C): $100,000 is not a recognized SE tax computation figure.
Wrong (D): $60,000 incorrectly applies a 50% reduction, which relates to the AGI deduction for SE tax, not the tax base.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Golden parachute payments Section 280G analysis
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Henley Industries is negotiating a change-in-control agreement with its CFO, Dana Whitfield. Whitfield's base amount (average W-2 compensation for the prior five years) is $400,000. The proposed agreement provides a lump-sum payment of $1,500,000 upon a change in ownership. The tax advisor must structure the agreement to avoid the Section 280G excess parachute payment consequences. Whitfield's total parachute payments would include the $1,500,000 plus $100,000 in accelerated option vesting. Evaluate the tax implications and recommend the optimal structure.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Pay the full $1,600,000 because the penalty only applies if parachute payments exceed four times the base amount", "Obtain a shareholder vote to approve the payment, which eliminates the Section 280G limitation regardless of the amount", "Cap the total payment at $1,199,999 to stay below the three-times-base-amount threshold and avoid the 20% excise tax", "Structure the payment as deferred compensation under Section 409A to avoid the parachute payment rules entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 280G. Parachute payments become excess parachute payments when total parachute payments equal or exceed three times the base amount (3 x $400,000 = $1,200,000). At $1,600,000, the excess is $1,200,000, subject to a 20% excise tax under Section 4999 and nondeductible to the employer. Capping total payments below the 3x threshold ($1,199,999) avoids both penalties entirely.
Wrong (A): The threshold is three times the base amount, not four times. $1,600,000 exceeds $1,200,000.
Wrong (B): Shareholder approval can exempt payments from 280G only for certain S corporations and private companies, not for all publicly held corporations.
Wrong (D): Section 409A deferred compensation does not override Section 280G; contingent-on-change-in-control payments are parachute payments regardless of deferral structure.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — NQSO withholding obligations at exercise
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Cascade Energy Corp issues NQSOs to plant manager Viktor Petrovic. He exercises 1,000 options at $15 per share when the FMV is $48 per share. What withholding obligation does the employer have at the time of exercise?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No withholding is required because stock options are not wages", "Withholding is required only for federal income tax, not FICA", "Withholding is required only for FICA, not federal income tax", "The $33,000 spread is subject to both federal income tax withholding and FICA taxes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 83; Treas. Reg. 1.83-6. The spread on NQSO exercise ($48 - $15 = $33 per share x 1,000 = $33,000) is treated as W-2 compensation subject to federal income tax withholding, Social Security tax (up to the wage base), and Medicare tax. The employer must withhold as it would on any other wage payment.
Wrong (A): NQSO spread at exercise is classified as wages subject to all employment taxes.
Wrong (B): Both federal income tax and FICA withholding are required, not income tax alone.
Wrong (C): Both FICA and income tax withholding apply, not FICA alone.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Constructive receipt doctrine
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Wentworth Consulting credits a $50,000 year-end bonus to partner Alicia Cheng's account on December 28, 2026. The funds are available for withdrawal at any time, but Cheng does not withdraw the money until January 5, 2027. In which year must Cheng include the bonus in gross income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["2026, because the income was constructively received when credited and made available without restriction", "2027, because income is recognized only when actually received in cash", "The income may be split between 2026 and 2027 at Cheng's election", "2026 only if Cheng is a cash-method taxpayer; 2027 if she is an accrual-method taxpayer"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 451; Treas. Reg. 1.451-2. Under the constructive receipt doctrine, income is taxable in the year it is credited, set apart, or made available to the taxpayer without substantial limitation or restriction, even if not actually received. Since the bonus was credited and available on December 28, 2026, it is includible in 2026.
Wrong (B): Actual receipt is not required; constructive receipt triggers inclusion.
Wrong (C): The constructive receipt doctrine does not permit income splitting across years.
Wrong (D): Constructive receipt applies to cash-method taxpayers, but the key point is that 2026 is the correct year regardless of method since the income was available.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Additional Medicare tax threshold
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$What is the additional Medicare tax rate imposed on earned income exceeding $200,000 for a single filer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["0.45%", "0.9%", "1.45%", "3.8%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 3101(b)(2). The Additional Medicare Tax of 0.9% applies to earned income (wages and SE income) exceeding $200,000 for single filers ($250,000 for MFJ). This is in addition to the regular 1.45% Medicare tax.
Wrong (A): 0.45% is not a recognized Medicare tax rate.
Wrong (C): 1.45% is the regular Medicare tax rate, not the additional surtax.
Wrong (D): 3.8% is the net investment income tax rate under Section 1411, not the additional Medicare tax on earned income.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Employer deduction timing for restricted stock
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Quantum Labs grants employee Dr. Anand Sharma 2,000 shares of restricted stock on January 1, 2026, when FMV is $30 per share. Sharma does not make a Section 83(b) election. The shares vest on January 1, 2029, when FMV is $75 per share. When does Quantum Labs receive its compensation deduction, and for what amount?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$60,000 deduction in 2026 when the shares are granted", "No deduction is available because restricted stock is equity compensation", "$150,000 deduction in 2029 when the shares vest and the employee recognizes income", "$90,000 deduction in 2029 for the appreciation only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 83(h). The employer's deduction for restricted stock equals the amount included in the employee's gross income and is taken in the employer's taxable year that includes the end of the employee's taxable year in which the income is recognized. Without an 83(b) election, Sharma recognizes $150,000 (2,000 x $75) at vesting, and Quantum Labs deducts $150,000 in 2029.
Wrong (A): Without an 83(b) election, no deduction arises at grant because the employee has no income inclusion.
Wrong (B): Employers do receive a deduction for restricted stock compensation when the employee recognizes income.
Wrong (D): The deduction equals the full FMV at vesting ($150,000), not just the appreciation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — S corp reasonable compensation for SE tax planning
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Kira Nakamura is the sole shareholder of Pinnacle Consulting, an S corporation. The business earns $350,000 of net income. Kira pays herself a $90,000 salary and takes $260,000 in distributions. A CPA reviews the arrangement and identifies a compliance risk. What is the most likely concern?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $260,000 distribution exceeds the annual distribution limit for S corporations", "S corporation shareholders cannot receive both salary and distributions in the same year", "Kira's salary is subject to double taxation at both the corporate and individual levels", "The $90,000 salary may be unreasonably low, exposing Kira to IRS reclassification of distributions as wages subject to employment taxes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 1366; Rev. Rul. 74-44. The IRS requires S corporation shareholder-employees to pay themselves reasonable compensation before taking distributions. A $90,000 salary on $350,000 of income for a consulting firm may be deemed unreasonably low, allowing the IRS to reclassify distributions as wages subject to FICA taxes. Courts have consistently upheld reclassification where compensation is disproportionately low.
Wrong (A): There is no annual distribution limit for S corporations; distributions are limited by stock basis.
Wrong (B): S corporation shareholders routinely receive both salary and distributions.
Wrong (C): S corporations are pass-through entities; income is not subject to double taxation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Year-end bonus 2.5-month rule Section 267
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Cedar Valley LLC, an accrual-method partnership, accrues a $100,000 bonus to managing partner Luisa Fernandez (a cash-method, related taxpayer under Section 267) on December 31, 2026. The bonus is not paid until April 15, 2027. In which taxable year may Cedar Valley deduct the bonus?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["2027, because Section 267 defers the deduction until the related party includes the amount in income", "2026, because accrual-method taxpayers deduct expenses when the liability is fixed and determinable", "2026, because the 2.5-month rule under Section 461 allows a deduction if paid by March 15, 2027", "The deduction is permanently disallowed because bonuses to related parties are nondeductible"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 267(a)(2). When an accrual-method payor owes compensation to a related cash-method payee, the deduction is deferred until the taxable year in which the payee includes the amount in income. Since Fernandez does not receive payment until April 2027, the deduction is taken in 2027, not 2026.
Wrong (B): The general accrual rule is overridden by Section 267 for related-party transactions.
Wrong (C): The 2.5-month rule under Section 461(h) applies to certain accrued liabilities but is superseded by Section 267 for related parties.
Wrong (D): The deduction is deferred, not permanently disallowed.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Economic benefit doctrine
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Meridian Technologies establishes a secular trust to hold $500,000 for its CEO's deferred compensation. The trust assets are beyond the reach of Meridian's general creditors. Under the economic benefit doctrine, when is the CEO taxed on the $500,000?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["When the CEO actually withdraws the funds from the trust", "Immediately, because the CEO has a current economic benefit from assets placed beyond the reach of the employer's creditors", "At the CEO's retirement date specified in the deferral agreement", "Only when the trust is dissolved and assets are distributed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 83; Economic Benefit Doctrine. When an employer funds deferred compensation through a trust where assets are beyond the reach of the employer's creditors, the employee has a current economic benefit and is taxed immediately on the funded amount. This distinguishes secular trusts (taxable upon funding) from rabbi trusts (assets subject to employer creditor claims, so no current taxation).
Wrong (A): Actual withdrawal is irrelevant when the economic benefit doctrine applies.
Wrong (C): The deferral agreement cannot override the economic benefit doctrine when assets are secured.
Wrong (D): Taxation occurs upon funding, not dissolution.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — ISO excess over $100K treated as NQSO
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Vertex Biotech grants VP of Research Dr. Elaine Okoye ISOs on January 2, 2026, for 8,000 shares at an exercise price of $18 (equal to FMV). All options first become exercisable on January 2, 2027. Dr. Okoye exercises all 8,000 options on March 15, 2027, when FMV is $40 per share. She sells all shares on June 1, 2028, for $52 per share. The tax advisor must determine the character of income at exercise and gain at sale. What is the correct analysis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All 8,000 options qualify as ISOs; no ordinary income at exercise and the entire $272,000 gain is LTCG at sale", "All 8,000 options are disqualified because the total exceeds $100,000; $176,000 ordinary income at exercise", "5,555 options qualify as ISOs (no regular tax at exercise) and 2,445 are treated as NQSOs with $53,790 ordinary income at exercise", "The $100,000 limit applies only to the spread at exercise, so all 8,000 options qualify as ISOs since the spread per share is under $100,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 422(d). The $100,000 ISO limitation is based on the aggregate FMV of stock (determined at grant date) for which ISOs first become exercisable in any calendar year. At $18 per share, 8,000 shares = $144,000 FMV at grant, exceeding $100,000 by $44,000. Only $100,000/$18 = 5,555 shares qualify as ISOs. The remaining 2,445 shares are treated as NQSOs, generating $22 spread x 2,445 = $53,790 ordinary income at exercise.
Wrong (A): The $100,000 annual ISO limit disqualifies a portion of the options.
Wrong (B): Only the excess over $100,000 of grant-date FMV is disqualified, not all options.
Wrong (D): The $100,000 limit is based on aggregate grant-date FMV of the underlying stock, not the per-share spread at exercise.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — ESPP Section 423 discount limit
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Marlow Industries offers an employee stock purchase plan (ESPP) under Section 423. The plan allows employees to purchase company stock at a discount. What is the maximum discount from fair market value that a Section 423 ESPP may offer to remain a qualified plan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["5%", "10%", "20%", "15%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 423(b)(6). A qualified ESPP under Section 423 may offer stock at a price not less than 85% of the FMV, which represents a maximum 15% discount. The price is determined using the lesser of FMV at the beginning of the offering period or at the purchase date.
Wrong (A): 5% is too low; the statutory maximum discount is 15%.
Wrong (B): 10% is below the allowable maximum; the statute permits up to 15%.
Wrong (C): A 20% discount would exceed the Section 423 limit and disqualify the plan.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Installment sale Section 453 income timing
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Tax advisor Monica Vance is counseling a client who sold appreciated business equipment for $200,000 with an adjusted basis of $80,000. The buyer will pay $50,000 per year over four years. The client wants to minimize current-year tax liability. Monica recommends reporting under the installment method. What is the gross profit percentage applied to each payment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["60%, resulting in $30,000 of gain recognized per annual payment", "40%, resulting in $20,000 of gain recognized per annual payment", "100%, because all gain must be recognized in the year of sale", "25%, resulting in $12,500 of gain recognized per annual payment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 453. Under the installment method, the gross profit percentage equals gross profit divided by the contract price: ($200,000 - $80,000) / $200,000 = 60%. Each $50,000 payment triggers $30,000 of gain recognition (60% x $50,000). This spreads the $120,000 total gain over four years.
Wrong (B): 40% would be the basis-to-price ratio, not the gross profit percentage.
Wrong (C): The installment method specifically allows deferral of gain recognition over the payment period.
Wrong (D): 25% confuses the number of payments with the profit ratio.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — 50% SE tax deduction effect on AGI
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Freelance architect Tomás Rivera earns $180,000 in net self-employment income for 2026. His calculated self-employment tax is $22,239. Rivera wants to understand how the SE tax deduction affects his individual tax return. What is the correct treatment of the deduction for one-half of self-employment tax?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It reduces the self-employment tax base, lowering the SE tax owed", "The deduction of $11,120 is an above-the-line deduction that reduces Rivera's AGI but does not reduce the self-employment tax itself", "It increases Rivera's AGI because SE tax is added back to income", "It is an itemized deduction on Schedule A"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 164(f). The deduction for one-half of SE tax ($22,239 / 2 = $11,120) is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040 that reduces adjusted gross income. However, it does not reduce the SE tax base or the SE tax liability itself. The deduction approximates the employer-equivalent FICA deduction that employees receive indirectly.
Wrong (A): The 50% SE tax deduction does not reduce the SE tax base; the 92.35% factor serves that purpose separately.
Wrong (C): The deduction reduces AGI; it is not added back to income.
Wrong (D): The deduction is an above-the-line (for AGI) deduction, not an itemized deduction on Schedule A.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Section 409A six permissible distribution events analysis
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Granite Holdings' NQDC plan currently permits distributions upon the following events: (1) separation from service, (2) disability, (3) death, (4) a specified date, (5) change in control, and (6) unforeseeable emergency. The board proposes amending the plan to add a seventh distribution trigger: the employee's written request with 90 days' notice. A tax advisor reviews the amendment. What is the correct advice?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The amendment is permissible if the plan document specifies the 90-day notice requirement", "The employer may add the trigger but must apply a 10% early distribution penalty similar to qualified plans", "The amendment would violate Section 409A because it effectively permits acceleration of benefits beyond the six statutory distribution events, subjecting all plan participants to immediate income inclusion and the 20% penalty", "The amendment is permitted under Section 409A as long as the employee has been in the plan for at least five years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 409A(a)(2). Section 409A limits distributions to six permissible events: separation from service, disability, death, specified time or schedule, change in control, and unforeseeable emergency. Adding an employee-request trigger constitutes an impermissible acceleration of benefits under Section 409A(a)(3), causing all vested deferrals to become currently taxable with a 20% additional tax and interest.
Wrong (A): A notice period does not cure the violation; the trigger itself is impermissible.
Wrong (B): Section 409A does not use a 10% penalty; the penalty is 20% plus interest.
Wrong (D): No service-duration exception exists for adding impermissible distribution events.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: easy / L2 — Dependent care assistance exclusion
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Harper & Associates provides employee Ben Calloway with $6,000 in dependent care assistance during 2026 through a qualified plan under Section 129. What amount must Calloway include in gross income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0", "$5,000", "$6,000", "$1,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 129(a). A qualified dependent care assistance program allows employees to exclude up to $5,000 per year ($2,500 if MFS) from gross income. Calloway received $6,000, so the excess of $1,000 ($6,000 - $5,000) must be included in gross income.
Wrong (A): Only $5,000 is excludable; the $1,000 excess is taxable.
Wrong (B): $5,000 is the excludable amount, not the includible amount.
Wrong (C): The full $6,000 is not taxable because the first $5,000 qualifies for the exclusion.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — ISO employee-only requirement
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Solaris Dynamics wants to grant incentive stock options (ISOs) to three individuals: an employee, an independent contractor who provides regular consulting services, and a non-employee board member. To how many of these individuals may Solaris validly grant ISOs?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only the employee, because ISOs may be granted only to employees under Section 422", "The employee and the independent contractor, because both perform services for Solaris", "All three, because ISOs may be granted to any individual providing services to the corporation", "The employee and the board member, because board members are treated as statutory employees"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 422(a)(1). ISOs may only be granted to individuals who are employees of the granting corporation (or its parent or subsidiary). Independent contractors and non-employee directors are ineligible. NQSOs may be used for non-employees.
Wrong (B): Independent contractors are not employees and cannot receive ISOs.
Wrong (C): Only common-law employees qualify for ISOs, not all service providers.
Wrong (D): Non-employee directors are not statutory employees for ISO purposes.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Section 4999 excise tax on excess parachute payments
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$DataCore Systems enters a change-in-control agreement with its CEO, Priya Deshmukh. Deshmukh's base amount is $500,000. Upon a qualifying change in ownership, she will receive total parachute payments of $1,800,000. A tax advisor must calculate the Section 4999 excise tax liability and assess the overall tax cost. What is the excise tax owed by Deshmukh, and what additional consequence applies to DataCore?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$100,000 excise tax on Deshmukh; DataCore loses the deduction for $500,000", "$260,000 excise tax on Deshmukh; DataCore loses the deduction for $1,300,000 of excess parachute payments", "$300,000 excise tax on Deshmukh; DataCore retains the full deduction but must pay a matching corporate penalty", "No excise tax because Section 4999 applies only to publicly traded companies"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Sections 280G and 4999. Total parachute payments ($1,800,000) exceed 3x the base amount ($1,500,000), triggering the excess parachute payment rules. The excess parachute payment is $1,800,000 minus the $500,000 base amount = $1,300,000. The Section 4999 excise tax is 20% x $1,300,000 = $260,000, owed by Deshmukh. Under Section 280G, DataCore loses its deduction for the $1,300,000 excess.
Wrong (A): The excise tax is $260,000 (20% of $1,300,000), not $100,000, and the lost deduction is $1,300,000.
Wrong (C): There is no matching corporate penalty; the employer consequence is loss of the deduction under Section 280G.
Wrong (D): Section 280G and 4999 apply to all corporations, not just publicly traded companies.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Cash method taxpayer timing strategies
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$CPA advisor Nolan Park is helping a cash-method sole proprietor who expects significantly lower income in 2027 than in 2026. The client has $40,000 of outstanding invoices from 2026 clients. Nolan recommends a timing strategy. Which approach would legally defer income from 2026 to 2027?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Backdate invoices to show a 2025 billing date", "Deposit December 2026 checks in January 2027 to avoid constructive receipt", "Delay sending invoices for completed 2026 work until January 2027, so clients pay in 2027", "Instruct clients to postdate checks to January 2027 even though they are delivered in December 2026"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 451; Treas. Reg. 1.451-2. A cash-method taxpayer recognizes income when received or constructively received. By delaying invoicing until January 2027, payments are not received or made available in 2026. This is a legitimate timing strategy because the taxpayer has no right to income that has not been billed.
Wrong (A): Backdating invoices is fraudulent and would not defer income.
Wrong (B): If checks were received in December 2026, the taxpayer has constructive receipt regardless of when deposited.
Wrong (D): A check received in December 2026 is constructively received at delivery, regardless of the date written on the check.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Stock appreciation rights taxation
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Waverly Corp grants 3,000 stock appreciation rights (SARs) to VP of Marketing Darius Holm on June 1, 2026, when the base price is $22 per share. The SARs vest and are exercised on June 1, 2029, when FMV is $58 per share. Holm receives a cash settlement. What are the tax consequences to Holm and Waverly at exercise?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Holm recognizes $108,000 of capital gain; Waverly receives no deduction", "Holm recognizes $108,000 of ordinary income subject to AMT only; Waverly deducts $108,000", "Both Holm and Waverly split the $108,000 tax burden equally", "Holm recognizes $108,000 of ordinary income subject to income tax withholding and FICA; Waverly deducts $108,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 83. SARs settled in cash are treated similarly to NQSOs. At exercise, the appreciation ($58 - $22 = $36 x 3,000 = $108,000) is ordinary income to Holm, subject to income tax withholding and FICA. Waverly receives a corresponding compensation deduction of $108,000 under Section 83(h).
Wrong (A): SAR income at exercise is ordinary income, not capital gain.
Wrong (B): SARs are not treated as ISOs and have no special AMT-only treatment; the income is subject to regular income tax.
Wrong (C): There is no shared-liability concept; the full $108,000 is income to Holm and a deduction for Waverly.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — On-premises athletic facility exclusion
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Orion Dynamics operates a fitness center on the premises of its corporate headquarters, available to all employees and their families. Membership at a comparable commercial gym costs $1,200 per year. Employee Jenna Torres uses the gym regularly. How much must Torres include in gross income for this benefit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0, because on-premises athletic facilities operated by the employer are excluded under Section 132(j)(4)", "$600, representing 50% of the commercial value", "$1,200, because gym access is a taxable fringe benefit", "$100, the de minimis fringe threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 132(j)(4). The value of an on-premises athletic facility operated by the employer is excluded from gross income if the facility is located on premises owned or leased by the employer, operated by the employer, and substantially all use is by employees, their spouses, and dependents.
Wrong (B): No 50% inclusion rule applies to employer-operated athletic facilities.
Wrong (C): On-premises employer-operated gyms are specifically excluded, unlike off-premises gym memberships.
Wrong (D): This exclusion is not limited by a de minimis threshold.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L3 — Phantom stock tax treatment vs restricted stock
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$NovaTech Solutions grants Senior Engineer Patricia Yun 5,000 phantom stock units tied to the company's share value of $30 per unit on the grant date. The units vest in three years, when the share value is $72. NovaTech pays Yun $360,000 in cash upon vesting. Separately, NovaTech grants another engineer 5,000 shares of actual restricted stock (same FMV and vesting schedule, no 83(b) election). How does the taxation of Yun's phantom stock differ from the restricted stock grant?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Phantom stock income is capital gain; restricted stock income is ordinary income", "Both generate ordinary income at vesting, but the phantom stock is NQDC subject to Section 409A while the restricted stock is governed by Section 83 and eligible for an 83(b) election", "Phantom stock and restricted stock have different FICA treatment: phantom stock is exempt from FICA while restricted stock is not", "Phantom stock and restricted stock are taxed identically with no legal distinction"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Sections 83 and 409A. Both phantom stock and restricted stock generate ordinary income at vesting. However, phantom stock involves no actual property transfer, making it nonqualified deferred compensation subject to Section 409A's timing and distribution rules. Restricted stock involves a transfer of property governed by Section 83, which allows a Section 83(b) election. Phantom stock has no 83(b) election because no property is transferred.
Wrong (A): Both phantom stock and restricted stock produce ordinary income, not capital gain.
Wrong (C): Both are subject to FICA taxes when income is recognized; there is no FICA exemption for phantom stock.
Wrong (D): The legal frameworks differ significantly: Section 409A governs phantom stock while Section 83 governs restricted stock.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Section 83 substantial risk of forfeiture
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Under Section 83, what creates a "substantial risk of forfeiture" for property transferred in connection with services?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The property is subject to a market-based risk of declining in value", "The property must be sold within a specified period", "The property is conditioned upon the future performance of substantial services or the occurrence of a condition related to the purpose of the transfer", "The employer retains a right of first refusal on the property"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 83(c)(1). A substantial risk of forfeiture exists when the rights to property are conditioned upon the future performance of substantial services or upon the occurrence of a condition related to the purpose of the transfer, and the possibility of forfeiture is substantial.
Wrong (A): Market-based decline in value is investment risk, not a substantial risk of forfeiture under Section 83.
Wrong (B): A requirement to sell does not constitute a risk of forfeiture.
Wrong (D): A right of first refusal alone does not create a substantial risk of forfeiture unless combined with a service condition.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — QBI deduction Section 199A basic application
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Elise Brannigan is a single taxpayer who earns $140,000 of qualified business income from her pass-through landscaping LLC (not an SSTB). Her taxable income before the QBI deduction is $155,000. She has no capital gains. What is her Section 199A qualified business income deduction?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$31,000", "$14,000", "$15,500", "$28,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 199A. The QBI deduction equals the lesser of (a) 20% of QBI = 20% x $140,000 = $28,000, or (b) 20% of taxable income before the QBI deduction (minus net capital gain) = 20% x $155,000 = $31,000. The lesser amount is $28,000. Since her income is below the threshold ($203,200 single in 2026), no W-2/UBIA limitation applies.
Wrong (A): $31,000 is 20% of taxable income, but the deduction is limited to the lesser of 20% of QBI or 20% of taxable income.
Wrong (B): $14,000 incorrectly uses 10% of QBI rather than 20%.
Wrong (C): $15,500 applies 10% to taxable income, which is not the correct rate.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — SE tax combined rate
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$What is the combined self-employment tax rate applied to net self-employment earnings?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["15.3%, consisting of 12.4% Social Security tax and 2.9% Medicare tax", "7.65%, consisting of 6.2% Social Security tax and 1.45% Medicare tax", "12.4%, consisting of Social Security tax only", "16.2%, consisting of 12.4% Social Security tax, 2.9% Medicare tax, and 0.9% additional Medicare tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Sections 1401(a) and 1401(b). Self-employed individuals pay both the employer and employee shares of FICA, totaling 15.3%: 12.4% for Social Security (Old Age, Survivors, and Disability Insurance) up to the wage base and 2.9% for Medicare (Hospital Insurance) with no cap.
Wrong (B): 7.65% represents only the employee's half of FICA, not the full self-employment tax.
Wrong (C): 12.4% is only the Social Security component; Medicare tax must also be included.
Wrong (D): The 0.9% additional Medicare tax applies only above the threshold ($200K/$250K) and is not part of the base SE tax rate.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Achievement awards Section 74(c)
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Ridgeline Manufacturing presents employee Jorge Medina with a gold watch worth $450 as a length-of-service award after 15 years of employment. The company has a qualified plan for employee achievement awards. Under Section 74(c), what is the maximum excludable amount for a qualified plan award?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$400", "$1,600", "$500", "$2,500"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 74(c). Employee achievement awards for length of service or safety are excludable from income up to $1,600 under a qualified plan ($400 if no qualified plan). Since Ridgeline has a qualified plan and the $450 watch is under $1,600, Medina excludes the full $450 from income.
Wrong (A): $400 is the limit for non-qualified plan awards, not qualified plan awards.
Wrong (C): $500 is not a recognized threshold under Section 74(c).
Wrong (D): $2,500 exceeds the statutory maximum for qualified plan achievement awards.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Meals and lodging Section 119
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Under Section 119, what conditions must be met for employer-provided meals to be excluded from an employee's gross income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The meals must cost less than $50 per day", "The meals must be organic, locally sourced, and approved by a nutritionist", "The meals must be furnished on the employer's business premises for the convenience of the employer", "The meals must be provided in lieu of a cash salary increase"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 119(a). Meals are excluded from an employee's gross income if they are furnished (1) in kind (not cash), (2) on the employer's business premises, and (3) for the convenience of the employer (i.e., a substantial noncompensatory business reason).
Wrong (A): There is no dollar-per-day limit for the Section 119 exclusion; the test is based on business purpose and location.
Wrong (B): Nutritional content has no bearing on the Section 119 exclusion.
Wrong (D): Meals provided as a substitute for cash compensation are compensatory and would not qualify for the exclusion.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Section 409A prior-year deferral election timing
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Under Section 409A, when must an employee generally make a deferral election for a nonqualified deferred compensation plan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Within 60 days after the compensation is earned", "At any time during the year the services are performed", "Within 30 days after the deferral amount is determined", "Before the beginning of the taxable year in which the services giving rise to the compensation are performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 409A(a)(4)(B)(i). A deferral election must generally be made no later than the close of the taxable year preceding the year in which the services are performed. This prevents taxpayers from deferring compensation after they already know the amount.
Wrong (A): A 60-day post-earning window is not permitted under Section 409A (except for newly eligible employees).
Wrong (B): Elections during the service year would violate Section 409A's anti-abuse timing rules.
Wrong (C): A 30-day post-determination window is not a recognized election period under Section 409A.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Social Security wage base cap
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$What is the effect of the Social Security wage base on self-employment tax?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The 12.4% Social Security portion of SE tax applies only to net SE earnings up to the annual wage base; earnings above that are subject only to the 2.9% Medicare tax", "Both the Social Security and Medicare portions of SE tax are capped at the wage base", "The wage base has no effect on SE tax because self-employed individuals are exempt from the cap", "The 12.4% Social Security tax applies to all SE earnings with no cap"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 1401. The 12.4% Social Security (OASDI) component of SE tax applies only to net SE earnings up to the annual Social Security wage base ($181,200 in 2026). Earnings above that threshold are subject only to the 2.9% Medicare tax (plus the 0.9% additional Medicare tax above $200K/$250K). There is no cap on the Medicare tax.
Wrong (B): Only the Social Security portion is capped; Medicare has no wage base limit.
Wrong (C): Self-employed individuals are subject to the same wage base cap as employees.
Wrong (D): The Social Security tax is capped at the wage base; it does not apply to all earnings.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Accelerate vs defer income based on projected rate changes
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Tax advisor Rachel Kwon is developing a multi-year compensation strategy for Dr. Alan Mercer, a surgeon earning $650,000 annually. Congress has enacted legislation increasing the top marginal rate from 37% to 42% effective January 1, 2028. Dr. Mercer has the option to: (1) accelerate $200,000 of 2028 income into 2027 through a bonus arrangement, or (2) defer $200,000 of 2027 income into 2028 through a Section 409A plan. He also has $80,000 of deductible expenses he can time freely between the two years. Assuming all other income remains constant, what is the optimal strategy?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Defer income to 2028 and accelerate deductions into 2027 to minimize both years' tax liability", "Accelerate the $200,000 income into 2027 at 37% and defer the $80,000 of deductions to 2028 when they offset income taxed at 42%", "Neither strategy produces savings because the total tax over two years is identical regardless of timing", "Defer both income and deductions to 2028 because the higher rate increases the value of deductions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Tax rate arbitrage. When rates are increasing, income should be accelerated into the lower-rate year and deductions should be deferred to the higher-rate year. Accelerating $200,000 into 2027 saves $10,000 in tax ($200,000 x 5% rate differential). Deferring $80,000 of deductions to 2028 saves an additional $4,000 ($80,000 x 5%). Total savings: $14,000.
Wrong (A): Deferring income into a higher-rate year increases total tax; deductions should be deferred, not income.
Wrong (C): Timing matters when marginal rates differ between years; the total tax is not identical.
Wrong (D): Deferring income to a higher-rate year increases the tax on that income, outweighing any deduction benefit.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
